  note         = {No. CV 75-2057-JWC (C.D. Cal. 1978). Ten Mexican-American women filed suit against Dr. Edward James Quilligan, chief of obstetrics at Los Angeles County-USC Medical Center, alleging coerced sterilization during labor or immediately post-delivery. Judge Curtis ruled against the plaintiffs, finding no proof of intentional racial discrimination and accepting language barriers and time pressures as explanations for consent failures. The ruling exemplifies the \textit{Washington v. Davis} intent-doctrine gating mechanism applied to medical coercion: absent a ``smoking gun'' of explicit discriminatory purpose, systematic coercion of a racially-identified group is constitutionally invisible. Settlement/regulatory outcomes: California's 1975 Informed Consent Law; federal regulations requiring interpreters for all sterilization consents in federally-funded programs.},
